<h2>The Mix of Properties Around Troy</h2>
<p>Roofing in Troy often means more than one roof per property. A typical call might cover a house plus a detached garage, a metal shop building or a barn.</p>

<h3>Homes in town</h3>
<p>In-town homes range from older single-story houses to newer builds. Many have asphalt shingle roofs that have already been through several hail seasons. Common problems include worn pipe boots, lifted shingles along ridges and edges, and gutters that overflow during heavy downpours.</p>

<h3>Acreage, barns and shops</h3>
<p>On properties outside town, metal roofs are common on barns, equipment sheds and shops, and more homeowners are choosing metal for the house as well. Exposed-fastener metal panels need periodic attention: fasteners back out as the metal expands and contracts in the heat, and the rubber washers under them break down in the sun. Loose screws and failed washers are a frequent source of slow leaks. Our <a href="/services/metal-roofing-temple-tx/">metal roofing</a> service covers repairs, re-screwing and new installations.</p>

<h2>Weather Exposure North of Temple</h2>
<p>Open land around Troy means fewer trees and buildings to break the wind. Straight-line winds from spring and fall storms hit roofs more directly, and hail can fall across wide stretches of farmland and town alike. Summer heat is just as harsh as in Temple, which ages shingles and dries out sealants. After a strong storm, look from the ground for missing shingles, lifted metal panel edges or debris on the roof, and never climb up to check.</p>
